        "Workload also differs by structure rather than simply by difficulty. The Norwegian national curriculum spreads assessment across three years of videregående with a mix of continuous coursework and final exams in a subset of subjects each year, decided partly by lottery (trekkfag). The IB Diploma concentrates six subjects, Theory of Knowledge, the Extended Essay and CAS into a fixed two-year package with heavy internal-assessment weighting throughout. Cambridge sits closer to a traditional exam-heavy model, subject by subject, on a fixed external series. None is simply easier; each rewards a different way of working across the two or three years.",
