  faqs: [
    { question: "How do I find a good IB tutor in Norway?", answer: "Matching on the exact school and subject level works far better than a generic search, since a public videregående DP class and a private international school's DP class run quite differently day to day. Tell us the school, current grades and where things are actually going wrong, and IB Gram proposes a specific tutor rather than a list to sift through. Reach ibgram24@gmail.com or +91 7439 368 115 on WhatsApp with those details." },
    { question: "Can lessons be scheduled to CET or CEST?", answer: "Yes. Every booking runs on whichever of Central European Time or Central European Summer Time currently applies, shifting automatically each March and October so a family never has to track the change themselves. Most Norwegian families choose weekday afternoons and evenings after school, or weekend mornings. Tell us the window that works and a tutor whose own hours fit it gets proposed." },
    { question: "How much does IB tutoring cost in Norway, and how do we pay?", answer: "Cost tracks the programme level, subject and weekly hours booked — DP Higher Level sciences and mathematics generally cost more per hour than MYP support. A specific rate is confirmed in writing before any booking, so the first invoice holds no surprise. Send your child's grade, subject and level to ibgram24@gmail.com, and raise currency and payment method in the same message." },
    { question: "Do you offer in-person tutoring in Norway?", answer: "No — every lesson for Norway runs online, and the only place we place a tutor in person is India, where the team is based. In practice that means live video, one-to-one, with a shared digital board for working problems and past papers, plus written notes to revisit afterward. This matters most for a smaller school's IB or IGCSE cohort, where a specialist in a specific HL subject often isn't available locally at all." },
    { question: "My child could study at a free public IB school or a private international one — does it matter for tutoring?", answer: "It affects the surrounding environment more than the subject content, since both routes teach the same Diploma and sit the same May exams. A public videregående DP class usually sits inside a larger Norwegian-medium school; a private international school runs English throughout the day with a more internationally mobile cohort. We tutor both and match a tutor who already understands the specific setting your child is in." },
    { question: "Can you help with Cambridge IGCSE if we live in Norway?", answer: "Yes. A number of Norway's private international schools, the International School of Stavanger among them, teach Cambridge IGCSE as a stage before the IB Diploma. We tutor Cambridge and Edexcel IGCSE subjects against the exact syllabus your child's school follows, using that board's own past papers and mark schemes. Send us the syllabus code from the course outline so we confirm the right variant and exam series." },
    { question: "Do your tutors teach both HL and SL subjects?", answer: "Yes, and the difference matters more than parents expect. HL courses carry genuinely extra content and harder, longer papers, so HL tutoring spends real time on that extension material and on managing timing under pressure. SL support usually concentrates on locking in the core syllabus and steady past-paper practice. Flag the level for each subject when booking." },
    { question: "Can a tutor help with my child's Extended Essay or internal assessment?", answer: "Yes, within limits the IB sets firmly. A tutor can help narrow a research question, walk through what the assessment criteria actually reward, sanity-check whether an argument holds together, and comment on a draft your child wrote themselves. A tutor cannot write, rewrite or hand over text for your child to submit as their own — the finished piece has to be theirs, and the school supervisor's guidance always overrides ours." },
    { question: "How do you check your tutors before matching one with my child?", answer: "Each tutor is interviewed on genuine subject depth and on the specific curriculum they will teach, backed by evidence of qualifications and prior IB or Cambridge classroom experience. We confirm they are current on this year's syllabus and marking criteria, not just broadly familiar with the subject. If a pairing turns out wrong once lessons start, tell us and a different tutor takes over." },
    { question: "Is there a trial lesson before we commit to anything?", answer: "Yes. An introductory lesson lets your child and a proposed tutor work through genuine material together before any block of lessons is booked. Use it to judge what actually matters: does the explanation style land, does the tutor clearly know your specific school's syllabus, and does the timing genuinely suit your household's afternoon or evening. Arranging a different tutor afterward takes one message if the fit isn't right." },
    { question: "Which IB and Cambridge subjects do you actually cover?", answer: "Across DP groups 1 to 5: both Mathematics routes, Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Economics, Business Management, History, Geography, Computer Science, English A and B, Norwegian A, Environmental Systems and Societies, plus Theory of Knowledge and matching MYP, PYP and Cambridge IGCSE support. Less common language or arts combinations depend on tutor availability, so send the exact subject and level from your child's timetable." },
    { question: "Do you offer intensive tutoring before the May IB exams?", answer: "Yes. Since most Norwegian schools sit the May session, the shift from one weekly lesson to two or three usually begins in February or March and builds through spring — full timed papers, markscheme walkthroughs, and a tight focus on whatever the mocks flagged as weak. Book that increase early; the spring calendar fills fastest every year." },
    { question: "Will an IB Diploma help my child get into a Norwegian university?", answer: "Generally yes, since a completed IB Diploma typically satisfies the educational side of generell studiekompetanse, the baseline qualification Samordna Opptak requires. The separate Norwegian-language requirement depends on whether Norwegian A or B was taken inside the Diploma, and bonus realfagspoeng points depend on whether HL subject choices match the currently approved list — both worth confirming directly with Samordna Opptak well before Vg1 subject choices are finalised." },
    { question: "Does my child need to know Norwegian to study the IB in Norway?", answer: "Not to follow DP lessons themselves, since IB subjects are taught and examined in English (or another approved language) regardless of school type. Norwegian only becomes relevant later, for the separate language requirement inside generell studiekompetanse if a family is applying to a Norwegian university through Samordna Opptak — a requirement students without Norwegian A or B in their Diploma need to check current alternative routes for." },
    { question: "How do we get started with a tutor?", answer: "Write to ibgram24@gmail.com or message +91 7439 368 115 on WhatsApp with your child's grade and school, the subjects and levels involved, hours that work on CET or CEST, and the specific outcome you're aiming for. From there we name a tutor, share their background, and set up a first lesson — nothing is charged until you've seen that lesson and agreed on a rate." },
  ],

